### 2. Files that will be pushed to GitHub

Your project should look something like this:

```text
birthday-website/
├── app/
│   ├── page.tsx
│   ├── layout.tsx
│   └── globals.css
├── public/
│   ├── images/
│   │   ├── photo1.jpg
│   │   ├── photo2.jpg
│   │   └── ...photo12.jpg
│   └── music/
│       └── happy-birthday.mp3
├── .gitignore
├── package.json
├── package-lock.json
├── next.config.ts
├── tsconfig.json
└── README.md
```

Your photos and music will be uploaded to GitHub because they're inside the `public` folder. Since this is a personal birthday website, remember that files committed to a public GitHub repository can be viewed or downloaded by others.

### 3. Push your project to GitHub

Open the terminal inside your project folder and run:

```bash
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit - Birthday Website"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/birthday-website.git
git push -u origin main
```

Replace `YOUR_USERNAME` with your GitHub username and create an empty `birthday-website` repository on GitHub first.

### 4. Verify `.gitignore` is working

Before committing, run:

```bash
git status
```

Make sure `node_modules`, `.next`, and `.env` are not included.

**Important:** If you previously added these files to Git, `.gitignore` alone won't untrack them. Run:

```bash
git rm -r --cached node_modules .next
```

Then commit again. If any environment secrets were previously pushed, remove them from Git history and rotate the exposed credentials.

Once pushed, your Next.js birthday website will be ready to connect to Vercel for deployment.